# A whole exchange in a clothes shop: what you want, the size, the colour, trying on and the price.
lesson language-clothes-shop v=1 format=teach grade=5 unit=9 subject=language levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "At the clothes shop"
  goal "Ask for clothes in a shop in [[language.name]], give a size and a colour, and say whether they fit."

  look {
    scene 24x8 {
      column all at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=start {
        row shop space=2 align=end {
          garment a kind=top colour=sky sleeves=long print=none pattern=stripes
          garment b kind=trousers colour=sky sleeves=long print=none pattern=plain
          sign s lines=["[[shop.sale|cap]]"] kind=hanging color=berry
        }
      }
    }
    say """
      In the shop, ask for what you want: [[shop.i-would-like-a-t-shirt-please]] The shop assistant
      asks [[shop.what-size]], what size, and [[shop.what-colour]], what colour. Ask
      [[shop.can-i-try-it-on]], can I try it on? If it does not fit, say [[shop.it-is-too-big]] or
      [[shop.it-is-too-small]] If it does, [[shop.i-will-take-it]], I will take it.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked language.clothes-shop-name k=4
    }
    show language.clothes-shop-name k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.clothes-shop-meaning k=4 easy-k=1
    show language.clothes-shop-write k=2 easy-k=1 hard-k=4
    show language.clothes-shop-name k=4 easy-k=1 hard-k=6
    show language.clothes-shop-match k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show language.clothes-shop-write k=3 easy-k=2 hard-k=5
    show language.clothes-shop-listen k=3 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.clothes-shop-say k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    level hard {
      show language.clothes-shop-write k=3
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show language.clothes-shop-why k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show language.clothes-shop-puzzle k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  remember {
    say "[[shop.i-would-like-a-t-shirt-please]] [[shop.what-size]] [[shop.what-colour]] [[shop.it-is-too-big]] [[shop.i-will-take-it]]"
  }

  grown-ups """
    The wrong idea to watch for is too big and too small taken for each other, and a question
    answered with the wrong thing, a size for a colour: the naming questions describe each moment of
    a visit to a shop. The prices on the list are made up for the sums, in euros as in Spain. The
    step up from the clothes and the prices of grade three is a whole exchange in a shop, with the
    assistant's questions answered in turn. From this grade the written words are marked with their
    accents: a word typed without its accent is not taken. The dictation is read from this sheet,
    where it prints as Read aloud, or played by the device. If the child is stuck, play shop with
    real clothes and a price label on each. Off the page, read a shop's sale sign in the language,
    or make one. It meets England's key stage 2 languages programme (years 3 to 6, statutory from
    age 7): "engage in conversations; ask and answer questions; express opinions and respond to
    those of others; seek clarification and help"; Japan's grades 5 and 6 foreign languages: "Can
    give instructions, make requests and respond to them by using basic expressions"; and CEFR A2,
    "Can understand sentences and frequently used expressions related to areas of most immediate
    relevance (e.g. very basic personal and family information, shopping, local geography,
    employment)". The phrases are in [[language.variety]].
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level [[shop.it-is-too-big]] is worked first in the teacher's pen, the words to name are [[shop.i-would-like-a-t-shirt-please]] and [[shop.what-size]] and the words to write are [[shop.changing-room]] and [[shop.sale]], the plainer ones of the lesson, with the first set to match, the shortest sentence to hear and the first version of each star question."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the words to name are [[shop.it-is-too-small]] and [[shop.how-much-is-it]] and the words to write are [[shop.jacket]], [[shop.jeans]] and [[shop.price]], the ones most often mixed up, with the last set to match, the longest sentence to hear, a third word to write and the last version of each star question."
  }
}
